WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.202
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:03.636
is an Emmy and
Academy Award-winning

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:04.904
writer, director, and comedian

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:05.972
behind films such as

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.674
"Get Out," "Us,"
and "Wendell &amp; Wild."

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.342
His critically-acclaimed
film "Nope"

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.277
is now streaming on Peacock.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.479
Please welcome back to the show,
our friend,

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:15.515
Jordan Peele, everybody!

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:18.518
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:25.525 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:32.565 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:34.867
-Welcome back!

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:38.504
It is so lovely to
have you here.

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:41.607
Last time you were here,
I was lucky to have you

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:43.843
with the entire cast of "Nope."
-Yeah.

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.678
-This time I'm very happy
to have you to myself.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:46.946
You know, we've known each other
for a very long time.

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:47.980
-Yes.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.050
We've come some far way,
my old friend.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.151
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:00:52.151 --> 00:00:54.620
We met each other about
20 years ago.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.722
We worked for
an improv comedy theater

00:00:56.722 --> 00:00:58.691
in Amsterdam called
Boom Chicago!

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.293
And I know we've talked
about that before,

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:01.894
but it's still, you know,
I can't believe it.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:03.730
I can't believe I was lucky
enough to know you then.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.399
I can't believe how incredible
your career has been since,

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.268
and it's really cool to be able
to sit here with you like this.

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.437
-And we had some wild times.
-We had some wild times.

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:12.372
-In Amsterdam.
-In Amsterdam, we did.

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:13.439
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.207
Mostly legal.

00:01:15.207 --> 00:01:16.443
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, right?

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:18.377
-I mean, everything
is legal there.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:19.813
That's the problem.

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:22.215
-Well, I was thinking because
there's a really cool thing,

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:24.183
which is the amusement park
in the film "Nope."

00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:25.484
Jupiter's Claim.
-Mm.

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:26.920
-Is actually being recreated
at Universal Studios.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.187
-Yes.

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:30.924
-One of the wilder times we had,
and more than once,

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:33.092
was we would go to
a Dutch theme park

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:34.026
called Efteling.

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:36.395
-Yes. "Elfland."
-Elfland, yeah.

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:39.698
You just translated the Dutch.
It's called "Elfland."

00:01:39.698 --> 00:01:41.834
And it's a wonderful place.
-Yeah.

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:44.537
-And even though there's not
a sign outside that says

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.272
"Do drugs here"...

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.373
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:48.707
...I always felt like
it was implied.

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:49.876
-It was implied.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:51.177
-And somehow accepted, like,

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:53.846
the staff would walk up to you
with shrooms

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:56.382
is kind of the feeling.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:58.117
They didn't have, like,
cotton candy.

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:01.321
Not, like, high-calorie
foods that American..

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:04.590
-Children running around
and they look like fawns.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.092
[ Laughter ]
Yes.

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.460
-Dutch fawns.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.096
-And it's interesting, because
the Dutch are a very --

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:12.465
very blunt people,
they're very honest.

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:14.834
They'll walk up to you and say,
"How nice to meet you.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.203
I don't like your shirt."
You know?

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.838
They'll say things like that.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:21.774
And I feel like there was
one moment

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:24.810
where you sort of
adopted that sort of tone.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.613
-I think because we were
in the Dutch culture

00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:30.816
and maybe I had
overserved myself.

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:32.218
I became very honest.

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:35.121
-To be fair, we had had
a long bacchanalia.

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:37.323
-Yeah.
-Of sorts. A long party.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:40.493
And I remember you saying to
a group of us --

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:43.129
-Dear friends of mine.
-Dear friends.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:45.364
Just straight up,
"I don't know, man.

00:02:45.364 --> 00:02:49.268
I don't want to be here anymore.
I don't want to.

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:50.503
I'm tired of hanging out with

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.472
the people
I'm hanging out with."

00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:54.574
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:55.808
And we're just watching you

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:57.843
feel comfortable
telling us that, you know.

00:02:57.843 --> 00:02:59.812
-It was a very supportive
group of people.

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:02.014
I think what had happened is,
I had gone to get a water

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:04.917
and I was standing in line,
and very amusement-park-y,

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:07.320
someone had won, like,
a stuffed lizard

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.288
and a child was holding
a stuffed lizard

00:03:09.288 --> 00:03:11.290
and the stuffed lizard
was looking at me.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.358
-And you had a meltdown.

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:13.326
-And I had a moment
where I'm like,

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:15.128
"I gotta go."
-Yeah.

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:16.729
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.998
And then we grow up.
-And then we grew up.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:20.466
-And now we're...

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:22.335
-But I didn't know this, of all
these years I've known you,

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:26.739
I did not know that your first
major in college was puppetry.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.674
-Yes.
-Wow.

00:03:28.674 --> 00:03:32.144
I mean, it does feel like
the good first building block,

00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:33.713
but what was the decision
to choose that?

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:36.015
-Well, yeah, so I went to
Sarah Lawrence College

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:38.250
in part because it was
mostly female.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.019
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:42.321
-Then I immediately took away
my advantage

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:44.323
by declaring myself
a puppetry major.

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:45.992
[ Laughter ]

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:47.526
To balance things out.
-Yeah, right.

00:03:47.526 --> 00:03:49.629
You don't want to
make it unfair.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:51.864
-I don't want to make it unfair.

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.165
But no, I was in love --

00:03:53.165 --> 00:03:57.170
I grew up and I loved
"The Nightmare Before Christmas"

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.938
growing up,

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:03.542
and how that film by
the great Henry Selick,

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:05.244
whom I've had the pleasure
of working with...

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:06.445
-Yeah, you just did a film,

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:08.347
"Wendell &amp; Wild"
with Henry Selick.

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:11.350
-"Wendell &amp; Wild," which is
the type of film that,

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:16.755
if I had seen when I was a kid
or if I was 12 years old,

00:04:16.755 --> 00:04:19.158
I think it would have changed
the way I feel about

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:23.729
my place in the industry
and in puppetry.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:25.898
And so, very proud of this film

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:27.833
and it's really --
it's wonderful, yeah.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:29.101
-I think one of the cool things

00:04:29.101 --> 00:04:31.670
is watching you get to
collaborate with people

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.174
that I know you've loved forever
and I've loved forever.

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:36.875
You're an EP on
this wonderful show,

00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:38.677
"Hunters" is coming back
for a second season.

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:40.479
Al Pacino is one of the people
in "Hunters."

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:42.781
-Oh, yeah.
-I mean, a legend.

00:04:42.781 --> 00:04:43.816
-Legend.

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:45.150
-How was your time
with Al Pacino?

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.820
-Well, admittedly brief.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.791
I've been in the presence
of this man only briefly,

00:04:52.791 --> 00:04:55.461
and he's hilarious.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:58.597
I mean, first of all, you are
in the presence of a guy

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:02.201
who is essentially a made man in
terms of cool, forever.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.602
-Yeah.

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:05.971
-And he an actor's actor.

00:05:05.971 --> 00:05:07.239
He's the consummate actor.

00:05:07.239 --> 00:05:09.975
And so he's, you know --

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:12.411
[ As Al Pacino ] "He's always --
He's fascinating."

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:13.479
[ Laughter ]

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.314
I don't really have
a good Pacino.

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:16.949
-Mine is very good,
I should warn you.

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:18.384
-Okay, yeah.

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:20.953
-Mine is based entirely on the
fact that he was nominated,

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:22.655
I believe maybe for "Hunters."

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.291
He was nominated for an award
in the first COVID year.

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:27.727
And my favorite thing that
happened during COVID

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:29.796
was seeing Al Pacino
in a Zoom room.

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:31.097
-Okay.

00:05:31.097 --> 00:05:33.032
-Because even in that little box

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:34.466
where he didn't know
where to look

00:05:34.466 --> 00:05:36.235
and it was not well-lit,

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:37.803
still the coolest dude
in the world.

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:38.837
-That's right.

00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:40.906
-And so my new
Al Pacino impression

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:42.041
is entirely based on that.

00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:43.209
You ready for it?
-Yeah, okay.

00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:45.444
-[ As Al Pacino ]
"Hey, I'm in the Zoom room!"

00:05:45.444 --> 00:05:46.778
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.778 --> 00:05:47.847
-That's good.
-I know, right?

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:49.382
-That's good.

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:51.384
[ Applause ]

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:53.018
-Thank you.

00:05:53.018 --> 00:05:54.286
-Very good.
-Thank you.

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:56.655
-Yeah, there was a moment there
in his career

00:05:56.655 --> 00:05:59.358
where I feel like
he started chewing gum.

00:05:59.358 --> 00:06:02.428
And he got a little Black.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:04.063
[ Laughter ]

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:06.699
A little bit Southern,
a little bit Black.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:10.570
And it was like he was
honing his Black --

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:11.837
'Cause from "Scarface,"
he already had us.

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:13.472
-Yeah.
-From Michael Corleone,

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:14.740
he already had us.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:19.645
But I feel like he's committed
to his Blackness.

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:22.181
-And it's really fun
when somebody like that --

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:24.349
because they can make
those choices

00:06:24.349 --> 00:06:26.185
and they've gone past the point

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:27.686
where anybody's gonna
go over and be like,

00:06:27.686 --> 00:06:29.321
"Maybe just bring it --
Dial it back."

00:06:29.321 --> 00:06:31.691
-He can get away with anything.
-He can just get away with it,

00:06:31.691 --> 00:06:33.892
and so those people,
those icons, those legends,

00:06:33.892 --> 00:06:35.227
those are the fun ones
to keep an eye on,

00:06:35.227 --> 00:06:36.762
'cause you're like, "Never know
where this is gonna go."

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:40.132
-He could come in full-on
Foghorn Leghorn

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:42.501
and fool everybody.
-Yeah.

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:44.036
-But no, he's a genius.

00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:46.805
And "Hunters," man.

00:06:46.805 --> 00:06:49.108
The season is incredible.

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:50.175
Incredible cast.

00:06:50.175 --> 00:06:51.411
I'm so proud of the show.

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:53.412
It's crazy and it's wild.
-That's great.

00:06:53.412 --> 00:06:55.047
I want to ask you a bunch more
about "Nope."

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:57.417
We'll be right back
with more from Jordan Peele!

